\newpage
\begin{verbatim}

%
% TRANSFORMATION TNoParticleInsertion
< m1: VERBPPROP{VERBPPROPrec1}
               [mu1,
                predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                             [head/I1::VERB{VERBrec1}[mu2],
                              mu3
                             ]
               ]
>

< m : VERBPPROP{VERBPPROPrec1}
               [mu1,
                predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                             [head/I1::VERB{VERBrec1}[mu2],
                              mu3
                             ]
               ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: VERBrec1.particle = 0
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >


&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TNoParticleInsertion
\item[* relevant examples:] buy $\rightarrow$ buy
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] Verbppropformation
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) TC:
ParticleSpelling
\item[* date of creation:] 19-01-88
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:]--
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] To let verbs without a particle pass this tc
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:] Particles might have been considered 
prepositions in the lexicon, but \\
1) some particles may not be (transitive) prepositions: apart, away etc. These 
particles are usually also adverbs. (Question: is this a real argument for the 
question in case???)\\
2) the ambiguity between particle and preposition is resolved right after the 
morphology in the surface parser (check on match between VERBrec.particle and 
PARTrec.key). Since particles have no record, this saves the carrying around of 
a lot of attributes in the m-rules (note that particles are removed at a very 
late state in the derivation).
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:] none
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]dna
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
